# CAO Azure topology

Review-only output from **gh cao deploy generate**. The solver derives its
dependency graph from TOSCA Simple Profile 1.3 node-template requirements using
a standard-library topological sort; no TOSCA or graph Go library is needed.
It does not parse arbitrary TOSCA or cloud archives. No Node.js, cloud calls,
App creation, deployments or job starts are involved.
Generation compiles every emitted `.bicep` file with Microsoft's official
`bicep build --no-restore --stdout` before publishing this folder. Compiler
errors or a missing Bicep CLI fail generation; warnings are reported.
This is syntax/type/linter validation, not live Azure deployment acceptance.

Start with [MANIFEST.md](MANIFEST.md) for Mermaid architecture and dependency
diagrams. [topology.yaml](topology.yaml) is the generated TOSCA service template;
topology.json is the deterministic requirement-graph projection, not a second
topology language. manifest.json binds the generated files by SHA-256.
AGENT-MCP.md and the architecture diagram show Actions agents' separate
bearer-token plus OIDC provenance path. It is not enabled by generation.

## Review and use

1. Complete [TODO.md](TODO.md) and [GITHUB-APPS.md](GITHUB-APPS.md).
   Keep this folder private; it contains identities and secret references,
   never secret values.
2. Follow the [Azure bootstrap guide](https://github.com/githubnext/gh-cao/blob/main/docs/deployment-azure.md#bootstrap-steps)
   for registry bootstrap, approved policy build contexts, separately built
   Functions/provisioning/ingestion images, reviewed digests and evidence.
   Bicep creates ACR, but does not build images.
3. Run `bash scripts/validate.sh` and
   `bash scripts/what-if.sh SUBSCRIPTION_ID RESOURCE_GROUP`.
   Review the exact Bicep, parameters and what-if before your separately
   approved deployment process. No generated script applies changes.
4. Wait for successful database provisioning before starting any database-owning
   role. {{if eq .Topology.Features.Acquisition "collector"}}Pin reviewed inventory before starting collectors. Do NOT start snapshot ingestion.{{else}}Run verified snapshot or deliberately empty ingestion only after provisioning.{{end}}
5. Verify health, readiness, signed-out API denial, allowed/disallowed OAuth
   sign-in, independent SCM denial, image digests and retention on every role.
{{if .Optional}}
6. Review [OPTIONAL-FEATURES.md](OPTIONAL-FEATURES.md). Optional hosts are a
   separate deployment AFTER the baseline. Their runtimes remain staged until
   policy, credentials and independent acceptance evidence are satisfied.
{{end}}

The Azure baseline's Bicep files are byte-for-byte copies embedded in the
generating CLI. Its manual ingestion job is provisioned even in collector mode;
presence does not authorize running it. Generated TOSCA DependsOn relationships describe
configuration/bootstrap prerequisites, NOT replacement ARM scheduling.

{{if eq .Topology.Features.Ingress "private"}}Private ingress disables public access. The baseline does NOT create a Functions
private endpoint, proxy, VPN, DNS or approved client connectivity. Arrange and
verify these externally, including OAuth callbacks and webhook delivery.
{{end}}
{{if eq .Topology.Features.Analytics "adx"}}The baseline's paid ADX destination does not enable export, create runtime
schema or grant an exporter. Azure Functions is not an exporter host.
{{end}}

## Checking and reproducibility

`gh cao deploy check /absolute/path/to/this-folder` re-solves requirements
against the generated TOSCA requirement graph, validates dependency closure/order/cycles
and feature/parameter/secret consistency, and detects file drift and orphaned output.
Keep the generating CLI release: newer embedded source revisions can intentionally
reject an older bundle. This is not a signed attestation or live readiness check.
Edit requirements.yaml and generate a NEW folder; do not hand-edit outputs.

The Azure baseline is experimental, not production-assured or a certified
service. This bundle is not the native deployment CLI's guarded apply plan.
External prerequisites remain unverified, and no authority is inferred from
credentials, topology validation or provisioning.
Turning features off does not tear down resources, stop existing runtimes,
revoke Apps or erase evidence; removal requires a separate reviewed procedure.
